================================================================================
NEW ISSUES / RESIDUALS found by this review
================================================================================
N1 (Medium, PRE-EXISTING, adjacent to [14]) — the stored `branch` is still trusted.
    ops.ts `removeWorktree` deletes `entry.branch` with `git branch -d|-D` and `mergeWorktree` merges
    `entry.branch`, but `store.ts readIndex` validates only field TYPES. A tampered
    `<ws>/.mpd/worktrees/index.json` (writable by any member, whose sandbox IS the parent workspace per
    D1) can therefore make the captain's `mpd_worktree_remove {force:true}` delete an unrelated local
    branch, or `mpd_worktree_merge` merge one. `git` protects only the branch checked out in the main
    worktree. Direction: revalidate `entry.branch` before use — `isValidBranch`, the branch `git -C
    <path> rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` actually reports for that worktree, and a refusal for
    `protectedBranches` / the main checkout's HEAD. Present at HEAD before this wave; NOT introduced by
    the fixes, and not what [14] named (it named the path), so it does not fail the wave.

N2 (Nit, NEW) — an ABSENT `cwdForMember` is treated as "no checkout existed".
    worktree.ts `memberWorktreeLookup`: `existed = typeof worktree.cwdForMember === "function" &&
    worktree.cwdForMember(...) !== undefined`. A partial service view without `cwdForMember` yields
    `created: true`, so a failed spawn releases a checkout that may have been REUSED. The throw path
    one line below already fails safe (`existed = true`). Direction: default `existed` to true when the
    probe is not a function.

N3 (Nit, NEW) — two wiring tests assert fake paths that contradict Lane A's layout.
    worktree-wiring.test.ts `pathFor` and c74-midrun-and-binding.test.ts's worktree double build
    `<dir>/<team>/<member>` from the RAW member name ("Senior Engineer"), but Lane A slugifies
    ("senior-engineer", proven by worktree.test.ts). The comments say "derived key/path exactly as Lane
    A builds it". The arms are still valid (they prove request passthrough) but the stated fact is
    wrong; a future reader can mistake the fake for the contract.

N4 (Nit, NEW evidence gap) — [1]'s production call site has no test.
    protocol.ts passes `worktreeTeamId: boardOf(caller.workspace, cell).teamId`; the only test supplies
    `worktreeTeamId` directly to `promoteAfterHandoff`, so a future edit that reverts the call site to
    `caller.cell.teamId` would keep the suite green.

N5 (Nit, NEW evidence gap) — the `add_member` official-executor arm is untested; only the approval
    site's official arm is (worktree-wiring.test.ts). The code path is symmetric with approval.

N6 (Nit, PRE-EXISTING) — `cwdForMember`/`createForMember` hand out a recorded path after `existsSync` +
    `isGitManaged`, without re-asserting `isUnderRoot`; D2 is enforced at CREATE only. A tampered index
    could redirect a member's cwd. Same trust boundary as N1.

N7 (Nit, PRE-EXISTING) — a leftover directory at the member path that git does not manage ("ghost")
    permanently blocks recovery for that pair: `createForMember` degrades (`path-exists`) on every
    attempt and never self-heals. Fail-closed and logged, but undocumented in docs/worktree.md's bounds.

N8 (Nit) — `effectiveConfig` reads each `worktree.*` leaf twice (typeof + value); harmless, but a
    service whose getter mutates could merge an inconsistent value.

================================================================================
DECLINED TO JUDGE (weighed, set aside — nothing silently dropped)
================================================================================
- Repo-containment refusing a workspace that is a strict SUBDIRECTORY of a git repo (no member
  worktrees at all there): CR-directed, safe-degrading (D4), and documented in all four docs. Not a
  defect of this wave.
- `const backend = deps.executor()` hoisted to once per approval (was once per member): the adapter's
  `teamExecutor()` is total and stable for a composition; no observable regression, and the worktree
  decision must use the same backend as the spawn.
- Cleanup through a nested `dsh.executeTool("mpd_worktree_remove")` rather than a service method: a
  legitimate use of Lane A's own tool; its refusal is logged, never fatal.
- An empty-but-successful roster read is cached as an empty set (so no member is classified read-only):
  indistinguishable from a genuinely empty roster at this layer; fail-open is the documented rule.
- `worktree.max: 0.5` defaulting to 32 instead of being REJECTED: the CR body's own remedy was
  "default values whose floored result is less than one"; a boot refusal would be worse.
- 152 pre-existing extensionless relative specifiers in the three touched packages: AGENTS.md §6's
  `packages/*/src` clause permits them and only the 5 CR-flagged NEW specifiers were changed (a
  whole-tree rewrite would violate the minimal-diff rule).
- A `writeTeam`/`updateTeamMember` throw AFTER a successful spawn would now also release the created
  worktree: the member row is already dropped on that path (pre-existing), so the child is orphaned
  either way; unreachable in practice (pure store writes).
- The `repo` tool-parameter description does not restate the containment rule: the refusal sentence
  names it and the docs carry it.
- Style: the team-core index.ts mixes `.ts` and extensionless specifiers; no gate covers specifier
  form, so this is cosmetic only.

================================================================================
BOUNDS — what this review did NOT verify
================================================================================
- No live team spawn and no live cellwork compaction reseed were driven end-to-end. [4], [6] and D8 are
  proven at the seam level (unit arms + the boot mount), not with a real child process.
- The Docker real-machine lane, the live-model QA cases, `dual-track-smoke` and `mcp-call` were not
  run (not requested; no credentials used).
- Only the PINNED harness 0.2.2-rc.1 was exercised; the also-supported 0.2.1-rc.5 was not.
- A repo-root `bun test` was not run (it scans `evidence/**` by design, §11); per-package suites were.
- `verify:vendor`, `verify:dshgo`, `verify:graph`, `verify:tui-admission`, `verify:workflows` ran only
  as `verify:gates` members (all green), not standalone with their `--self-test` arms.
- No Windows/macOS path/symlink semantics were tested (Linux only).
- Only CodeRabbit round 1 was reconciled: 18 inline comments + the single additional comment in that
  review body (review id 5869177628). Later rounds' threads were not part of this wave.
- Code outside the 22 changed files was read only where the change's blast radius required it; the
  remaining ~640 TS files were not re-reviewed.
- Test-quality judgement is limited to the new/changed arms; the pre-existing 1,500+ assertions were
  run, not re-derived.
